 \begin{abstract}
 \bfseries\boldmath
-Protein-protein interaction (PPI) graphs can add molecular context to missense variant prediction, but their contribution depends on evaluation design and baseline strength. We audit fixed two-hop propagation of ESM protein embeddings using 177,213 ClinVar variants and a STRING association graph. Only downstream classifiers are trained. Across three seeds, propagation increased mean AUC from 0.7835 to 0.7950; protein-clustered intervals for the reference-model gains included zero. Connectivity and topology controls showed performance sensitivity, although their propagation and training settings were not fully matched. With CADD and AlphaMissense included, protein-representation controls retained much of the fusion gain. Under homology-aware splitting, the single graph-fusion estimate was below the repeated strong-predictor baseline. Topology groups and error transitions showed exploratory associations rather than established correction mechanisms. BioGRID second-network validation did not establish a consistent added benefit over raw-embedding controls. These findings support context-dependent protein-representation complementarity and careful evaluation of network-specific increments. They concern fixed propagation and should not be generalized to trained graph neural networks.
